Virtual functions allow derived classes to override methods in the base class. The key idea is to ensure that the function call
is determined by the type of the object being pointed to rather than the type of the pointer or reference.
This concept is tightly linked to inheritance, where a base class type can refer to a derived class object. 
Without virtual functions, the method call is resolved based on the pointer's type, not the object it points to.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base{
    public:
    void print(){
        cout<<"Base ..."<<endl;
    }    
};

class Derived:public Base{
    public:
        void print(){
            cout<<"In derived ..."<<endl;
        }
    
};

int main() {
    // Write C++ code here
    Base b;
    Derived d;

    b.print(); //calls the print function of the Base class.
    d.print(); //calls the overridden print function in Derived.
//However, bp->print() calls the Base class function because bp is a Base pointer, even though it points to a Derived object. 
    //This happens because the print function in the base class is not virtual.
    Base *bp=&d;
    bp->print();
    
    return 0;
}
